  "narrative": "During the 1930s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture were deeply rooted in their cultural heritage. The afro-textured hair of many individuals was often styled into distinctive patterns, such as the afro itself, which was created by combing out natural growth or using chemical curling products to enhance curls. Cornrows, a traditional hairstyle from Africa, were also popular among Black and African women, who braided their hair close to the scalp in intricate designs. Protective hairstyles, designed to minimize manipulation and exposure of Afro-textured hair to environmental elements, likely played a significant role in hair care during this era. However, documentation from this specific decade is limited in our source material, so we can only speculate on the prevalence of these styles and their cultural significance. Despite this limitation, it is clear that Black and African women's hairstyles were an integral part of their identity and cultural expression during the 1930s.",
